+Reading from a file
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+The :func:`~rich.progress.open` function opens a file and shows a progress bar that advances as the file is read. The default mode is ``'r'``, which is text: ``read()`` returns ``str``. Use ``'rb'`` to read binary data, in which case ``read()`` returns ``bytes``. The default total is the size of the file in bytes. Here's an example::
+
+    from rich.progress import open
+
+    with open("data.json") as text_file:
+        text = text_file.read()
+
+    with open("data.bin", "rb") as binary_file:
+        data = binary_file.read()
+
+The progress display starts when the ``with`` block is entered, and stops when it exits. The file is closed on exit.
+
+If you already have a Progress instance, call :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.open` to read a file into one of its tasks. Unlike the module-level function, this method does not start the display, so enter the Progress as a context manager. Here's an example::
+
+    from rich.progress import Progress
+
+    with Progress() as progress:
+        with progress.open("data.csv", "rb", description="Loading") as csv_file:
+            csv_data = csv_file.read()
+
+Reading from a file-like object
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+If you already have an open binary file-like object, use :func:`~rich.progress.wrap_file` or :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.wrap_file`. Only binary file objects are supported. You must give a ``total`` (the number of bytes to be read) because the size can't be determined from a file-like object. The reader does not close the file you pass in. Here's an example::
+
+    import io
+
+    from rich.progress import wrap_file
+
+    source = io.BytesIO(b"some data")
+    with wrap_file(source, total=9) as reader:
+        data = reader.read()
+
+With :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.wrap_file`, you can instead give an existing task with a total, and omit ``total``. The task's total is used. A ``ValueError`` is raised if neither a total nor a task with a total is given. Here's an example::
+
+    import io
+
+    from rich.progress import Progress
+
+    source = io.BytesIO(b"some data")
+    with Progress() as progress:
+        task_id = progress.add_task("Parsing", total=9)
+        reader = progress.wrap_file(source, task_id=task_id)
+        data = reader.read()
+        reader.close()
+
